{"author":{"id":"1e0a6ff3e01313072cf7b3803c158c22d01a37717237fe198e814e781752b172"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1784646303,"nonce":"IjJkmN5rCSmYnJVHsfKuVRFrgGs=","message":"Root-cause analysis (no fix proposed). Two facets.\n\n(b) Not visible: the resolved base is never rendered in the series detail view\n(`_show_details`, `src/b4/review_tui/_tracking_app.py:1962-2092`, which shows\nsubject/status/change-id/link/target-branch but no base). It is only read at\napply time (`_tracking_app.py:2765`). This overlaps f41b565 (Displaying apply\ntargets).\n\n(a) Unexpected base: when a series has no stored `base-commit`, apply silently\nfalls back to the *current* HEAD of the target branch\n(`_tracking_app.py:2765-2773`: `git rev-parse \u003ctarget_branch\u003e`). Because the\nbase is resolved lazily at apply time rather than pinned when the user sets it,\nany movement of the target branch between selection and apply changes the base\nactually used -- including movement from concurrent workers (startup rescan\n`_tracking_app.py:876`, CI checks `_common.py:346`) or from another take onto\nthe same branch. That lazy-resolution-against-a-moving-HEAD, combined with the\nbase never being surfaced, explains the \"lag\" and the surprising base Mark\nsees. (I did not find a proven read/write race on a single base field; the\nconcrete cause is lazy resolution + no UI surfacing, with concurrency as a\ncontributing factor.)\n\nRelated: f41b565.","files":null}]}